Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
so  these jackets are beautiful richard ford  has an eye for detail  sizing up the inventory at franco womo a  menswear boutique in san jose there's  something traditional but there's also  something very contemporary about the  color and the pattern you know that's  not your dad's  jacket as ford knows when it comes to  fashion there's so much more than meets  the eye  he shares that insight in the book dress  codes how the laws of fashion made  history  dress codes are rules about what people  can wear so employers or schools or  sometimes even the government might have  rules or laws regulating the things that  people can wear  but i also mean by dress codes the way  we decode the meaning of clothing and  attire because clothing is communicating  and it is signifying you know social  status identity social position  a self-proclaimed close horse ford is a  professor of civil rights and employment  law at stanford  and a surprising number of legal  disputes and litigation revolves around  dress codes women suing their employers  who require women and only women to wear  makeup or high-heeled shoes african  americans sue their employers over dress  codes that prohibit certain hairstyles  dress codes that forbid certain types of  clothing that  religious traditions require people to  wear like head coverings and as ford  uncovers it's a story as old as fashion  itself  when did clothing go from just putting  something on to fashion  in the book i talk about the birth of  fashion in the late 1300s before then  clothes were draped on the body think  togas or robes that's until tailoring  became  widespread among elites and that changed  the nature of clothing and fashion it  made it much more expressive it made it  much more personal  but not everyone was permitted to dress  for success so-called sanctuary laws  kept royal subjects in their place going  back to the 1500s under queen elizabeth  you knew your place in society based on  what you were able to wear and what you  were wearing and this was true all over  europe but in tudor england and  especially under queen elizabeth the  first they were what were called acts of  apparel that regulated clothing  according to social status and they were  quite detailed only  a knight of the guarder or higher in  rank would be allowed to wear a certain  type of silk or a certain color or a  certain fabric and there were gradations  all the way down the social spectrum for  centuries all the sartorial advances  were tailored to men all the way up to  the business suit it's practical it's  sensible and that reflects the ideals of  enlightened reason and also of  industriousness a new value at this  period of time and what's important  about that moment in the 1700s and some  historians describe it as the great  masculine renunciation so it's the  renunciation of all of the finery and  what have you but it is a masculine  enunciation to some extent women's  clothing perhaps becomes even more  elaborate in opposition to this new  streamlined male silhouette it would  take another 120 years for a similar  revolution in the female wardrobe the  flappers although i think from today's  perspective we tend to think of them as  frivolous we think of them dancing in a  nightclub right  yeah exactly cocktails daisy buchanan  from the great gatsby but in fact the  flappers started off as working class  women  racially diverse group of women women  who needed clothing to be practical and  wanted to have some of the freedom of  men the 20th century put fashion at the  forefront of many social movements from  the zoot suit in the 1940s to the  refined attire of the civil rights era  if you go back in history there were  laws in the united states that  prohibited negroes and slaves from  dressing above their condition so when  the civil rights protesters wore their  sunday best where they knew they would  be confronted by racist mobs that was  part of the symbolism of the demand for  dignity and the demand for respect that  sense of fashion resonated with ford's  father he trained as a tailor  when he was in college a birmingham  alabama native who used clothing to  distinguish himself professionally so he  would always examine suits he would pick  them up and  and he'd check to make sure that there  was a  floating what's called a floating canvas  you'd pull between and you know if it  wasn't there he could no not that one  that's glued together um and then when  he found one like this that was very  nice you know he well  okay maybe for men suits remain the  dominant dress code in offices for  centuries but from wall street to  silicon valley dressing down is now  business as usual  so the dress code has gone away in the  workplace and particularly around here  in the silicon valley this is what you  need to wear to work that includes  everything from yoga pants to hoodies  it may seem like there's nothing to it  but the rejection of the business suit  the rejection of formal attire that was  a big deal in the early silicon valley  tech companies and they were pretty  explicit about thinking that rejecting a  formal dress code was a way of rejecting  the uptight lockstep non-creative ethos  of older corporate america yeah think  outside the box don't trust the guy in  the suit right exactly this one piece of  clothing doesn't just have one set of  coded language or coded meaning because  so much depends on social context as  well it matters whether you're wearing  it in the middle of the silicon valley  or you're wearing it in the inner city  yeah all of those things change the  meaning of clothing for this casual now  where are we in 50 years right well i  imagine the casual trend is here to stay  but i talked to the creative director  from esquire and he said you know the  new generation is wearing jackets and  ties and it's exactly as you suggested  my dad wears a sweatshirt and sneakers  and so i do see a new generation of  young people who are dressing in a bit  more of a refined manner

